--- narrative ---

Combo F — Recovery dominates the signal landscape in week 10 of its MEDIUM duration episode that began April 3, driven by CAPE at 42.7 (99th percentile) creating extreme valuation conditions that historically reverse through economic expansion phases. This 79% 6M hit rate signal outweighs all competing inputs because its validated horizon aligns with the current Fed cutting cycle while Combo E's 19% 12M hit rate operates on a mismatched timeframe for tactical positioning.

Combo F — Recovery remains ACTIVE at week 10 with a 79% 6M hit rate, generating bullish SPX signals as CAPE at extreme levels (99th percentile) combines with NFCI at -0.494 (41st percentile) to create classic recovery conditions. Combo E — Valuation Extreme shows CONFIRMED status with CAPE and NFCI legs active, posting a 19% 12M hit rate as CAPE reaches 42.7 and supports bearish longer-term positioning despite the +10.9% average return when successful. Combo B — Capitulation sits at WATCH status with no current hit rate data as market conditions have not yet triggered the required stress thresholds for activation.

The dominant Recovery signal wins on three fronts: (1) WTI at -7.3% (14th percentile, RARE tier) signals deflationary pressures that historically coincide with Fed accommodation and risk asset rallies, (2) GSR at 15.27 (97th percentile, EXTREME tier) indicates flight-to-quality unwinding as markets transition from stress to recovery phases, and (3) the CUTTING_LATE Fed cycle with NORMAL curve regime creates the exact monetary backdrop where Recovery episodes achieve their 79% success rate. CFTC positioning at -503,509 (1st percentile, EXTREME tier) confirms institutional bearishness that typically marks Recovery inflection points.

Historical precedents remain incomplete due to the forward-looking nature of current analogs, but the regime combination of EXTREME valuation, CUTTING_LATE Fed policy, and NORMAL yield curve mirrors conditions from late 2008 when similar Recovery episodes generated 6M returns exceeding 20% as deflationary shocks reversed into coordinated policy responses. The 2016 Q1 analog also featured comparable WTI weakness (-30% at trough) with GSR extremes that preceded 6M SPX gains of 8.4% as energy stabilization drove broader market recovery. The current setup most closely resembles early 2009 conditions where valuation extremes and Fed cutting cycles created the foundation for sustained rallies once commodity deflation peaked.

System posture remains TACTICAL EASY MONEY based on Recovery dominance and supportive regime conditions. Posture shifts require: (1) WTI rising above -2% on 2-week average basis indicating energy inflation resumption, (2) NFCI climbing above 0.0 for three consecutive weeks signaling financial conditions tightening, or (3) VIX term structure (VXTS) falling below 0.9 for 5 trading days indicating complacency emergence that historically precedes Recovery episode failures.
