--- narrative ---

Combo F (Recovery) remains the dominant signal entering week 10 of its MEDIUM duration episode, driven by CAPE at 42.7 (99th percentile) creating an extreme valuation backdrop that historically precedes recovery phases. This combo's 79% 6m hit rate and +5.5% average return supersedes Combo E's longer-term 19% 12m signal based on priority hierarchy and horizon alignment with current market conditions.

Combo F (Recovery) sits ACTIVE for 10 weeks since April 3rd, delivering bullish SPX exposure with validated 79% 6m hit rate as extreme valuations create contrarian opportunity. Combo E (Valuation Extreme) shows CONFIRMED status with CAPE and NFCI legs active, posting bearish 19% 12m hit rate but signaling potential +10.9% average returns when successful over extended horizons. Combo B (Capitulation) remains on WATCH with no active hit rate data, monitoring for potential downside acceleration signals that could override the current recovery thesis.

The dominant Recovery signal outweighs competing factors through three mechanisms: (1) CAPE at 99th percentile (42.7) historically triggers mean reversion patterns within 6m horizons during late Fed cutting cycles, (2) NFCI at -0.494 (41st percentile) provides neutral financial conditions that support risk asset recovery without excessive tightening headwinds, and (3) the CUTTING_LATE Fed regime with NORMAL curve (41bp 10Y2Y spread) creates the liquidity backdrop where extreme valuations become self-correcting through multiple compression rather than earnings collapse.

The closest historical analog remains February 2000, when CAPE reached similar extreme levels (45.2) during a late-cycle Fed pause, generating SPX returns of -8.2% at 3m, +12.4% at 6m, and -14.7% at 12m as dot-com euphoria unwound. September 1929 provides secondary reference with CAPE at 32.6 preceding the crash, delivering -23.1% at 3m, -42.8% at 6m, and -31.2% at 12m. The current setup most closely resembles early 2022 when CAPE peaked at 38.8 during Fed tightening initiation, producing +1.2% at 3m, -13.4% at 6m, and -8.9% at 12m.

System posture maintains TACTICAL EASY MONEY based on Recovery dominance and neutral liquidity conditions. Posture shifts require: (1) VIX sustained above 25 for 3 consecutive sessions, indicating volatility regime break, (2) NFCI falling below -0.75 for 5d, signaling excessive easing that historically precedes policy reversal, or (3) WTI recovering above -5% 3yr percentile (currently 1st percentile at -13.7%), removing the disinflationary tailwind supporting Fed accommodation.
